\title{The Topological Structure of Proof Dependency Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 13, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A mathematical proof induces a directed graph whose nodes are statements and whose
edges encode the direct-dependency relation ``$a$ is used in the proof of $b$.'' The
prohibition against circular argument is precisely the condition that this graph is
\emph{acyclic}: no statement is reachable from itself along a nonempty chain of
dependencies. We develop the topological consequences of this single hypothesis for
a finite collection of statements. Modeling a proof abstractly as an \emph{acyclic
relation} --- a binary relation whose transitive closure is irreflexive --- we prove
a self-reinforcing chain of results. Acyclicity forbids cycles of every length, not
merely self-loops and $2$-cycles. The \emph{ancestor set} of a node is strictly
monotone along reachability, yielding a \emph{topological numbering}: an integer rank
function that strictly increases along every dependency. From the extremes of this
rank function we obtain the existence of \emph{foundational} (source) and
\emph{capstone} (sink) statements in every nonempty finite proof graph. Finally,
asymmetry of the dependency relation gives a \emph{sparsity} bound: a proof graph on
$n$ statements has at most $n(n-1)/2$ direct dependencies. We discuss algorithmic
realizations, applications to software builds, scheduling, and spreadsheet
evaluation, and outline extensions.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Mathematics is cumulative. Each theorem is established by appeal to earlier
theorems, definitions, and axioms; those in turn rest on still earlier material.
Assigning to each statement a node and drawing an edge from $a$ to $b$ whenever $a$
is invoked in the proof of $b$ produces a \emph{directed graph of proofs}.

This graph obeys one structural law: there are no circular arguments. One cannot
legitimately prove $A$ from $B$ and $B$ from $A$; such an argument establishes
nothing. Translated to the graph, no directed cycle exists --- the graph is a
\emph{directed acyclic graph} (DAG).

We ask: what does acyclicity alone force upon the structure of a proof? We answer
with a chain of theorems, each building on its predecessors. The results apply
verbatim to any finite acyclic dependency structure, whether it arises from a
mathematical theory, a software module hierarchy, a task schedule, or a spreadsheet.

\section{Definitions and model}

Let $V$ be a type of \emph{statements} (the vertices), and let $R$ be a binary
relation on $V$, written $R\,a\,b$ or $a \to b$, meaning ``$a$ is used directly in
the proof of $b$.'' We call $R$ the \emph{direct-dependency relation}.

\begin{definition}[Reachability]
The \emph{reachability relation} $R^{+}$ is the transitive closure of $R$:
$R^{+}\,a\,b$ holds iff there is a nonempty chain
$a = x_0 \to x_1 \to \cdots \to x_k = b$ with $k \ge 1$. We then say $a$
\emph{reaches} $b$, or $a$ is a \emph{strict ancestor} of $b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Acyclic relation]
$R$ is \emph{acyclic} if $R^{+}$ is irreflexive:
$\forall v \in V,\ \neg\, R^{+}\,v\,v$. Equivalently, no statement is reachable
from itself along a nonempty chain --- there is no circular argument.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Ancestor set]
For $v \in V$, the \emph{ancestor set} is
$\Anc(v) = \{\, u \in V : R^{+}\,u\,v \,\}$. When $V$ is finite, its cardinality
$|\Anc(v)|$ serves as our rank function.
\end{definition}

We assume from Section~\ref{sec:mono} onward that $V$ is finite.

\section{Acyclicity forbids cycles of every length}

\begin{proposition}[No self-loops]
If $R$ is acyclic then $R\,v\,v$ fails for every $v$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
A single edge $v \to v$ witnesses $R^{+}\,v\,v$, contradicting acyclicity.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Asymmetry of $R$]
If $R$ is acyclic and $R\,a\,b$, then $R\,b\,a$ fails.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
From $a \to b \to a$ we obtain $R^{+}\,a\,a$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Asymmetry of reachability]
If $R$ is acyclic and $R^{+}\,a\,b$, then $R^{+}\,b\,a$ fails.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If both held, transitivity of $R^{+}$ gives $R^{+}\,a\,a$, contradicting
acyclicity.
\end{proof}

\section{Monotone ancestor sets and the topological numbering}
\label{sec:mono}

Assume $V$ finite.

\begin{lemma}[Ancestors captured by reachability]
If $R^{+}\,a\,b$ then $a \in \Anc(b)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]
If $R^{+}\,a\,b$ then $\Anc(a) \subseteq \Anc(b)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $u \in \Anc(a)$ we have $R^{+}\,u\,a$; with $R^{+}\,a\,b$, transitivity yields
$R^{+}\,u\,b$, so $u \in \Anc(b)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[No node is its own ancestor]
If $R$ is acyclic then $a \notin \Anc(a)$ for every $a$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$a \in \Anc(a)$ means $R^{+}\,a\,a$, forbidden by acyclicity.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Strict monotonicity]
If $R$ is acyclic and $R^{+}\,a\,b$, then $\Anc(a) \subsetneq \Anc(b)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Containment is Monotonicity. It is proper since $a \in \Anc(b)$ (as $a$ reaches $b$)
while $a \notin \Anc(a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Topological Numbering Theorem]
Let $R$ be acyclic on a finite type $V$. There is a rank function
$f : V \to \mathbb{N}$ with
\[
  R^{+}\,a\,b \;\Longrightarrow\; f(a) < f(b) \qquad \text{for all } a,b \in V.
\]
In particular $f(a) < f(b)$ for every direct edge $R\,a\,b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $f(v) = |\Anc(v)|$. If $R^{+}\,a\,b$ then $\Anc(a) \subsetneq \Anc(b)$ by strict
monotonicity, and a proper subset of a finite set has strictly smaller cardinality,
so $f(a) < f(b)$. Direct edges are a special case since $R\,a\,b \Rightarrow
R^{+}\,a\,b$.
\end{proof}

\section{Foundations and capstones}

\begin{theorem}[Foundation Theorem --- source]
Let $R$ be acyclic on a finite nonempty type $V$. Then there exists $v$ with
$\neg\, R\,u\,v$ for all $u$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $f$ be the rank function and $v$ a minimizer of $f$. If $R\,u\,v$ held then
$f(u) < f(v)$, contradicting minimality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Capstone Theorem --- sink]
Let $R$ be acyclic on a finite nonempty type $V$. Then there exists $v$ with
$\neg\, R\,v\,u$ for all $u$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Symmetric: take $v$ maximizing $f$.
\end{proof}

\section{Sparsity of proof graphs}

Let $n = |V|$ and $E = \{\,(a,b) : R\,a\,b\,\}$.

\begin{theorem}[Sparsity Theorem]
If $R$ is acyclic on a finite $V$ with $n = |V|$, then
\[
  2\,|E| \le n(n-1), \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad
  |E| \le \binom{n}{2} = \frac{n(n-1)}{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix the rank function $f$. Let
$A = \{(a,b) : f(a) < f(b)\}$ and $B = \{(a,b) : f(b) < f(a)\}$. The swap
$(a,b) \mapsto (b,a)$ is a bijection $A \to B$, so $|A| = |B|$, and $A, B$ are
disjoint subsets of the $n(n-1)$ ordered pairs of distinct vertices, giving
$|A| \le n(n-1)/2$. Every edge $(a,b) \in E$ satisfies $f(a) < f(b)$, so
$E \subseteq A$ and $|E| \le |A| \le n(n-1)/2$.
\end{proof}

This is the directed analogue of the classical $\binom{n}{2}$ bound: a consistently
directed dependency structure cannot be dense.

\section{Algorithms and applications}

The proofs are constructive. Setting $f(v) = |\Anc(v)|$ requires an
all-pairs reachability computation; more efficiently, \emph{Kahn's algorithm}
repeatedly removes an in-degree-$0$ vertex (a source, guaranteed by the Foundation
Theorem) to produce a topological order in $O(n+m)$ time and detect cycles.
Processing nodes in topological order computes the longest dependency chain
(\emph{proof depth} / critical path).

These structures appear throughout computation: software build graphs, spreadsheet
formula dependencies, project schedules (PERT/CPM), version-control commit graphs,
data pipelines, and curriculum prerequisites. In each, acyclicity yields a
consistent order, guaranteed endpoints, and a sparsity ceiling.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

Natural extensions include: antichain decompositions and Mirsky's theorem
(quantifying proof depth); a characterization of unique topological order via
Hamiltonian paths; transitive reduction isolating essential dependencies; weighted
critical-path models of verification cost; and the \emph{spine of mathematics}
conjecture, that the global dependency graph concentrates on a small set of
foundational hubs.

\section{Conclusion}

From the single premise that proofs cannot be circular, we derived that every finite
body of mathematics can be ranked by an integer topological numbering, must possess
foundational and capstone statements, and can contain at most $n(n-1)/2$ direct
dependencies. The prohibition on circular argument is a powerful structural
principle that shapes the whole edifice it governs.

\end{document}

